 "observations": "In this run we observed the same working pattern in every session after the first: the agent read the whole package with cat before editing (ck2 2.3-2.6, ck3 3.2-3.5, ck4 4.2-4.8, ck5 5.2-5.10), so every edited place was first seen in a read-all sweep (L3), including modules it never edited (I5, e.g. ck4 4.4 engine.py). Most of each change was new modules (sources/inputs/quality ck3 3.8-3.12; ingest/joins ck4 4.16, 4.18; versioning/udfs/checkpoints/metadata ck5 5.15-5.24), with existing functions changed at signatures or call points. At ck4 it extracted shared helpers (resolve_named_schema, 4.14; inputs.expand, 4.15) rather than copying. Verification was ad-hoc scripted runs on scratch fixtures in /tmp in every session (ck2 2.16, ck4 4.22, ck5 5.28); it never wrote unit tests. It ran spec examples (ck2 2.15, ck4 4.21), explicit Part-1/older-mode regression checks (ck2 2.16, ck4 4.26, ck5 5.33), pyflakes (ck3 3.24, ck5 5.35), and compared its own xxh64 against the xxhash library (ck5 5.20). It wrote notes for later sessions at ck1 (DESIGN.md, 1.2), ck2 (memory file, 2.19) and ck5 (5.38). All failures were coded F2: the full outer join drops the unmatched-side row with a null projected timestamp, as the agent tested deliberately (ck4 4.28); a malformed timestamp row is rejected (ck3); a config directory is not recursed without a recursive key (ck4, ck5); and an empty-input sum returns null where the ck5 test expects 0 (ck5)."
